From: nuno407 <11352538+nuno407@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 09:46:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/45] regulator: tps65910: don't clear external sleep control
 on power-off either

Power-off hung the same way as restart: the SoC froze in tps65910_shutdown()
before the PMIC power-off handler (DEV_OFF) ran, leaving the board powered
(USB still on) until a long press of the power button. Switching off through
the PMIC brings it up from a clean state on the next power-on, and the
firmware sets up the external control lines again at boot.
---
 drivers/regulator/tps65910-regulator.c | 11 ++++++-----
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/regulator/tps65910-regulator.c b/drivers/regulator/tps65910-regulator.c
index f31104d07..80d1e66ae 100644
--- a/drivers/regulator/tps65910-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/tps65910-regulator.c
@@ -1227,12 +1227,13 @@ static void tps65910_shutdown(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	int i;
 
 	/*
-	 * A restart goes through the PMIC itself (DEV_OFF_RST), which resets
-	 * these registers anyway. On the Microsoft Surface RT, whose vdd_cpu
-	 * is under EN1 external control, clearing the external control here
-	 * hangs the SoC before the restart handler runs.
+	 * Restart and power-off go through the PMIC itself (DEV_OFF_RST,
+	 * DEV_OFF), which brings these registers up from a clean state. On the
+	 * Microsoft Surface RT, whose vdd_cpu is under EN1 external control,
+	 * clearing the external control here hangs the SoC before the restart
+	 * or power-off handler runs.
 	 */
-	if (system_state == SYSTEM_RESTART)
+	if (system_state == SYSTEM_RESTART || system_state == SYSTEM_POWER_OFF)
 		return;
 
 	/*
